ot  yeah initially there was um just a  handful of people and it was you know  but but  because the sequences were so long the  team grew eventually i think we had over  20 animators working on the previous we  previous the whole film  um  so we were sharing sharing sequences and  we realized that  because alfonso was really pushing  not necessarily the details of the  animation but i mean he's obsessive with  composition and timings of the camera  and all the nuances of that were really  pushed in the previous were you  surprised at how  beautifully they wove together these  discreetly precise stagings into one  continuous flowing piece well it it it  had it was gradually worked up actually  you know and and that was one of the  reasons why as the movie we went on  we did less and less storyboarding i  think we storyboarded the whole movie  but we we relied more and more on  previews and the previews in many ways  was the spine of the movie from the  beginning to the end but um you know  however useful the storyboards are as an  initial starting point and they are  because so much of it is about movement  and timing and and all the things we've  been talking about we we  moved into previews uh you know sooner  as we as we went down the process and  also we found we had to previous to a  higher level of detail because so many  things were relying on the previous so  the previous had to be  you know worked up to  much more accurate animation than  previous normally is  because of the way we shot it  we were able to explore the  dimensionality  of  zero gravity in a way that you couldn't  in a normal traditional shooting because  you know normally in a film people are  standing on the ground so this makes  everything kind of two-dimensional  people can move left and right but it's  you know often to move up and down so  the compositions  are very kind of locked within that you  know we're used to seeing people framed  in a certain way  and you know one of the big briefs  is that the compositions should break  with that  um traditional structure i mean i  remember when we were doing the previous  everybody was you know we were having  fun  doing all this stuff and everybody was  saying well you know actually we've got  to film that and that was almost like an  inconvenience you know  we sort of tried to not think about it  and give alfonso just say look just  let's just go and make it have fun  try and you know come up with some cool  sequences and then leave it up to tim to  work out how we were going to shoot it i  have to say tim was very very gracious  because also editorially we were  manipulating all these previews and i  was spinning things around and flopping  the shot and moving stuff around and  re-timing things with the purposes of  the edit and i knew that every time i  did it tim would be receiving this edit  a few minutes later and banging his head  against the brick wall because of what i  just done
 

